k a  bit about that problem in the United  States do we have enough daycare  facilities for children of working  parents well Martha could probably  comment more expertly on the only  federal program we now have for daycare  which became a part of the special  services under Social Security and it  dealt with those on welfare or potential  welfare recipients it's always been my  view that beyond that government had a  responsibility to provide educational  services to young children before school  age and that really we didn't have but  600,000 spaces in the entire country for  early childhood education and this was  far too few when there were already six  million preschoolers whose parents  either single or both worked and  therefore we were falling far behind in  providing this very very needed service  one of the problems that we have with it  is that the states or the bureaucracy  itself  I want to write in very special  regulations I remember when I was a  member the state legislature that they  wanted to have very special regulations  for the buildings which made it almost  prohibitive now in many instances they  want to have very special regulations or  what the child is to eat and so forth  and so on so we have great difficulty  with it but we do need daycare and we're  giving it most in  portably in this country to people i  some of the very poor can get it not all  by any means but some but then the  middle class can't get it at all they're  not given exactly the same basis as  others we if you're going to have it my  opinion it should be given to everybody  alike and we should make arrangements to  do it but it shouldn't cost the same  amount as sending a child to Harvard  don't you think that's really good one  of the things it's so difficult to  explain is that we have a daycare  program under 4a of our Social Security  Act those on welfare the minute Thormann  goes to work she loses that opportunity  for care for her preschooler and so  while we're saying the whole program is  designed to get women to go to work and  to be self-sufficient and get off  welfare it isn't a zone of course Oh  welfare program discourages women from  going to work the truth is at the  welfare program is quite Congress of the  Congress of the United States has done  for women and let me tell you it's not  good right we are saying to them when  you are caught in this trap you are  there forever  we by this means you protect jobs for  men theoretically of course you don't  really because if you just permitted  those women to have jobs and have a  decent way to rear their children you  would have a greater demand upon the  entire economic structure and we can it  well afford it what chances do you  foresee in the near future Congress  really grappling with some of these very  central issues that it already did Wendy  on the daycare question in 1971 the  Congress in a very prolonged debate over  the issue for four years finally passed  a comprehensive a child care bill which  was vetoed by President Nixon and set  back the whole program tremendously and  it's taken us until now  December 71 to to this current time  where we're now again beginning to  regroup and decide we're going to go it  again I think with the new Congress  coming in and many new faces many more  women involved in politics in the local  level there is a far greater awareness  of the importance of this issue and so  I'm looking forward to the 94th Congress  really enacting a bill which will be the  first for our country and really it's  it's almost too late most of the  countries of the world have child care  programs certainly all Scandinavia in a  good part of Europe and Asia have  recognized the importance of providing  adequately for young children it's only  our country I think Martha that has  failed to recognize this responsibility  perhaps in International Women's year  this is a good news I really grappled  with yes in the 1974 elections women  scored significant gains at all levels  of government in fact more women than  ever before were elected to political  office women have had the vote in this  country since 1920 why now are they  doing so much better what why not before  well I think one of the answers is that  they have never run in such numbers and  they have never realized before the  exact issues now many of them do they  are well informed on the issues and they  are running many of them are running as  Patsy and I did with the support of our  husbands I bet they are trying and I  think that it will change American  politics I always thought it was wrong  that women would not vote for women they  do but so do men vote for women and  there were no women in America in  Watergate and I think this was a big  help  I will change politics women's  participation in greater numbers in the  political arena  do you agree first of all mrs. mink well  yes I agree  I think well you have to recognize it  took us 150 years to get the vote yeah  and 50 years is really in terms of time  not a great deal in terms of developing  from the just gaining the right to vote  the important position that I think so  many women hold in their respective  communities we may not have the numbers  but I think women I really listen to if  my state is typical we have many in the  legislature and they are the leaders  they they are in the forefront of public  opinion in the middle of public  controversies they're not afraid to  express themselves and I think it's  going to make a tremendous difference  it's going to I think just shake up the  system and make it far more responsive  to the human questions that confound and  and confuse the electorate as a whole so  I'm really very excited by it  and like Martha says our difficulty has  been that not enough of run we can't  expect every woman because she's a woman  the minute she runs she's going to be  successful and that's not possible so we  do need to have the numbers in there  competing and given the numbers I think  we're going to be more and more  successful over the years and one of the  ways in which is it changes it is it  permits a different viewpoint on every  law I can remember that we were once  arguing some years ago over who was to  get the jobs in a particular make-work  program and one of the women was  suggesting that they'd be 50 percent for  women 50% per man and one man rose on  the floor of the house and said what do  you mean it has to be given to the  breadwinner  who is the breadwinner  the only real poverty left in America  outside of the agent are in those  families that are supported by women so  that that woman needs to have that  opportunity of that job but you see you  have to have some women there to  understand this many of the laws have  been made have been made on the theory  that men will always hold these jobs  there for the surviving Widow was given  us a pension I changed the law that  permitted it to be the surviving spouse  if Patsy and I had died our husbands  would not have gone  and our pensions but the men's widows  picked them up at nine o'clock the next  morning or finally we change that but  there were all kinds of laws like this  you both didn't have combined it seems  to me very successfully marriage and  politics what do you say to people that  say the two might be incompatible have  you done this well I think that's  probably the the most offensive question  that's ever asked because I truly  believe that men and women are equal and  that just as it's difficult for a man in  politics to have the kind of relaxed  family life and leisure situation in  politics it's the same problem for women  it's really no different and I've never  heard anyone ask a man how has it been  on your family I mean it's not asked now  because women are getting into politics  and the question has become more  prevalent you see stories in Time and  Newsweek on the suffering wives yes men  politician right but it used to be  Martha th