d never heard  of Patsy Mink  I'll say well it's because of all of  your attitudes that drove men to  politics if you give me a job when I  came home from Rossville I would have  been really happy this is the royalty  check each what would you have much the  same I am story I was married and my  husband was in the law school and for  some reason he rather that law school  seemed to think well you know he will  take care of her and it's all right in  fact they had the nerve to say in front  of the other women in the law school  there were very few  well mrs. Griffiths is the first woman  that's ever come here that didn't come  to find a husband and one of the  Schieffer girls and Whittier said well I  think you're right and as a matter of  fact I've looked them all over and I'm  thinking of transferring  I thought was very good but I originally  I you know  they had the attitude well mr. Griffiths  will see to it that she's alright she's  known being trouble which was nonsense  well you believe this is changing now oh  yes easier probably yes  I think the law schools in particular  very conscious of the fact that they  have excluded women capable intelligent  women who would otherwise qualify for  enrolment except for their sex and are  beginning to expand their admission  policies and more and more women are  having an opportunity for a professional  education in all fields not just law  what do you see as the impact not just  on women but also on men and children of  the women's movement well I think in a  way that the women's movement has been  or has been a result of the impact of  changing times that is more education  for women but more divorce in this  country women have to look at the fact  that they're going to have to be  responsible for themselves the old myths  just don't work so that I in place of it  being the women creating an impact they  are reacting to an impact I think in it  I think this is done a good deal for men  and in the country right it's given them  a greater sense of freedom and  independence well before they had this  notion that in order to be quote a  gentleman in our society they had to  have quote a dependent wife you know  that they would display at cocktail  parties and and nothing beyond that I  think now they're sensing their  responsibility and entirely different to  context and both partners in a marriage  I think are looking upon themselves as  people and trying to do the best they  can with their abilities of talents one  last question and we're running short on  time so it's a very broad question maybe  a little unfair to be asking it but how  do you feel that women in this country  compared with women abroad in terms of  rights and responsibilities  and of course I realized that we depend  on which area of the world we're talking  about but could you generalize well I  have had members of the parliamentary  body of developing nations in Africa  come in to me and say oh I can't believe  it we have more rights than the women of  America have and we were told that the  women in America had everything and of  course we have nothing like the rights  of European women I think that's  absolutely true even with the Asian and  continent so many people say to me well  with your Asian background and all the  Asian traditions how could you possibly  broken the barrier you know and I look  at them and I say really they're much  farther ahead in Asia they have some  customs that maybe you don't understand  but when you move those customs aside  but women are very dominant in Asian  cultures and lawyers and have a very  good mastery of the situation whether  they are out in the front may may be a  question or two but I think that they're  very important figures who make a  significant contribution in their  societies well thank you both so much  we've been speaking with two  distinguished United States Congress  women Patsy Mink of Hawaii and Martha  Griffiths of Michigan on the subject of  women's 