ted and  I would hope that the Congress would  just get about the business of repeating  some of these laws before the actual  ratification but when it occurs it will  be an absolute necessity in my state for  instance we were the first to ratify er  a following that we have had to take a  systematic look chapter by chapter in  our revised laws and make all the  changes it just doesn't occur  automatically and so the status of  women's Commission for instance in my  state has made it their number-one  priority to just cleanse the laws of  everything they regard as being sex  discrimination this does not happen by  and of itself merely because we have  ratified er a or have a provision  similar to it in our state constitution  and so I suppose the honor of us bill I  put in would serve that purpose even  after ratification so what you're saying  is the Equal Rights Amendment applies to  federal law but then we have the states  have eyes also to state law oh it does  it applies at a local level to the local  school board can no longer say that in  the case of a depression first all women  teachers will be fired or all married  women will be fired and then will fire  the men it applies anyplace where  government acts with the knees or the  government acts but it would not apply  to private business that's right it does  not apply to industry so no what what is  the United States doing in this regard  well we have the 1964 Civil Rights Act  that applies to them and then of course  the in the higher education bill of  several years ago applied to women is  that right could you discuss that mrs.  Newton  yes that was with reference to any  policies and regulations  hiring this includes hiring firing  policies of the education departments  and the local communities as well as  universities this is a very important  step forward and quite complicated and I  guess there is a growing awareness among  educators that they haven't been  entirely fair  not only and who becomes an  administrator and who gets to be  principal and who gets to be the deputy  superintendent of curriculum but  generally in the way they have  administered their educational policies  so I'm fully in support of the amendment  that we put into the higher ed bill and  then of course this past Congress the  women's educational equity Act which was  a bill that you sponsored and worked  very hard for was recently signed into  law could you talk about this the need  for this legislation yes it's always  been my belief that no matter how many  laws we passed or how many  constitutional amendments we were  successful in having ratified that the  major problem in any society was the  attitudes that people grew up with or  were made to believe were secret  traditions of their civilization and so  long as any part of our society adheres  to a sexist notion that men should do  certain things that women should do  certain things and then begin to  inculcate our babies with these notions  through curriculum development and so  forth then we'll never be rid of the  basic causes of sex discrimination so  the women's educational equity provision  which is designed to provide monies for  small groups institutions women's  organizations school systems  universities whatever to try to a  grapple with this problem do some very  intensive work in curriculum revision  textbooks why do all the women for  instance in a child's Primmer  have to be a picture as homemakers with  the aprons on and the kid  never anything very exciting beyond  being a nurse the doctor is always a man  the lawyer the engineer the statesman is  always a man and this is the kind of  very subtle way in which in my view  girls and women are discouraged from  fulfilling their potential and so I  suppose the purpose of my bill is really  to free the human spirit to make it  possible for everyone to achieve  according to their talents and wishes  another subject which concerns you is  day care legislation could you talk a  bit about that problem in the United  States do we have enough daycare  facilities for children of working  parents well Martha could probably  comment more expertly on the only  federal program we now have for daycare  which became a part of the special  services under Social Security and it  dealt with those on welfare or potential  welfare recipients it's always been my  view that beyond that government had a  responsibility to provide educational  services to young children before school  age and that really we didn't have but  600,000 spaces in the entire country for  early childhood education and this was  far too few when there were already six  million preschoolers whose parents  either single or both worked and  therefore we were falling far behind in  providing this very very needed service  one of the problems that we have with it  is that the states or the bureaucracy  itself  I want to write in very special  regulations I remember when I was a  member the state legislature that they  wanted to have very special regulations  for the buildings which made it almost  prohibitive now in many instances they  want to have very special regulations or  what the child is to eat and so forth  and so on so we have great difficulty  with it but we do need daycare and we're  giving it most in  portably in this country to people i  some of the very poor can get it not all  by any means but some but then the  middle class can't get it at all they're  not given exactly the same basis as  others we if you're going to have it my  opinion it should be given to everybody  alike and we should make arrangements to  do it but it shouldn't cost the same  amount as sending a child to Harvard  don't you think that's really good one  of the things it's so difficult to  explain is that we have a daycare  program under 4a of our Social Security  Act those on welfare the minute Thormann  goes to work she loses that opportunity  for care for her preschooler and so  while we're saying the whole program is  designed to get women to go to work and  to be self-sufficient and get off  welfare it isn't a zone of course Oh  welfare program discourages women from  going to work the truth is at the  welfare program is quite Congress of the  Congress of the United States has done  for women and let me tell you it's not  good right we are saying to them when  you are caught in this trap you are  there forever  we by this means you protect jobs for  men theoretically of course you don't  really because if you just permitted  those women to have jobs and have a  decent way to rear their children you  would have a greater demand upon the  entire economic structure and we can it  well afford it what chances do you  foresee in the near future Congress  really grappling with some of these very  central issues that it already did Wendy  on the daycare question in 1971 the  Congress in a very prolonged debate over  the issue for four years finally passed  a comprehensive a child care bill which  was vetoed by President Nixon and set  back the whole program tremendously and  it's taken us until now  December 71 to to this current time  where we're now again beginning to  regroup and decide we're going to go it  again I think with the new Congress  coming in and many new faces many more  women involved in politics in the local  level there is a far greater awareness  of the importance of this issue and so  I'm looking forward to the 94th Congress  really enacting a bill which will be the  first for our country and really it's  it's almost too late most of the  countries of the world have child care  programs certainly all Scandinavia in a  good part of Europe and Asia have  recognized the importance of providing  adequately for young children it's only  our country I think Martha that has  failed to recognize this responsibility  perhaps in International Women's year  this is a good news I really grappled  with yes in the 1974 elections women  scored significant gains at all levels  of government in fact more women than  ever before were elected to political  office women have had the vote in this  country since 1920 why now are they  doing so much better what why not before  well I think one of the answers is that  they have never run in such numbers and  they have never realized before the  exact issues now many of them do they  are well informed on the issues and they  are running many of them are running as  Patsy and I did with the support of our  husbands I bet they are trying and I  think that it will change American  politics I always thought it was wrong  that women would not vote for women they  do but so do men vote for women and  there were no women in America in  Watergate and I think this was a big  help  I will change politics women's  participation in greater numbers in the  political arena  do you agree first of all mrs. mink well  yes I agree  I think well you have to recognize it  took us 150 years to get the vote yeah  and 50 years is really in terms of time  not a great deal in terms of developing  from the just gaining the right to vote  the important position that I think so  many women hold in their respective  communities we may not have the numbers  but I think women I really listen to if  my state is typical we have many in the  legislature and they are the leaders  they they are in the forefront of public  opinion in the middle of public  controversies they're not afraid to  express themselves and I think it's  going to make a tremendous difference  it's going to I think just shake up the  system and make it far more responsive  to the human questions that confound and  and confuse the electorate as a whole so  I'm really very excited by it  and like Martha says our difficulty has  been that not enough of run we can't  expect every woman because she's a woman  the minute she runs she's going to be  successful and that's not possible so we  do need to have the numbers in there  competing and given the numbers I think  we're going to be more and more  successful over the years and one of the  ways in which is it changes it is it  permits a different viewpoint on every  law I can remember that we were once  arguing some years ago over who was to  get the jobs in a particular make-work  program and one of the women was  suggesting that they'd be 50 percent for  women 50% per man and one man rose on  the floor of the house and said what do  you mean it has to be given to the  breadwinner  who is the breadwinner  the only real poverty left in America  outside of the agent are in those  families that are supported by women so  that that woman needs to have that  opportunity of that job but you see you  have to have some women there to  understand this many of the laws have  been made have been made on the theory  that men will always hold these jobs  there for the surviving Widow was given  us a pension I changed the law that  permitted it to be the surviving spouse  if Patsy and I had died our husbands  would not have gone  and our pensions but the men's widows  picked them up at nine o'clock the next  morning or finally we change that but  there were all kinds of laws like this  you both didn't have combined it seems  to me very successfully marriage and  politics what do you say to people that  say the two might be incompatible have  you done this well I think that's  probably the the most offensive question  that's ever asked because I truly  believe that men and women are equal and  that just as it's difficult for a man in  politics to have the kind of relaxed  family life and leisure situation in  politics it's the same problem for women  it's really no different and I've never  heard anyone ask a man how has it been  on your family I mean it's not asked now  because women are getting into politics  and the question has become more  prevalent you see stories in Time and  Newsweek on the suffering wives yes men  politician right but it used to be  Martha that only our poor husbands were  going on but it was a question with  regard to families and how difficult it  is to adjust I don't think our problem  has been any different than a male  politicians I agree with you and let me  give you one little incident we were  invited my husband died recently the  White House and my husband related for  dinner and my husband really didn't want  to go and I said well why not  he said because some woman will sit  beside me who will say and what do you  do for a living and when I say I'm a  lawyer he will say she will say and what  kind of law do you practice and she will  come very close to saying and how much  do you make and I said well when she  asked you that question why don't you  say to her and what do you do for a  living and if she says well I have eight  children why don't you say well I mean  other than that  you know why do you assume that a man of  a woman politician isn't doing anything  he's probably highly successful or she  wouldn't be a politician don't you think  it's right but it's infuriating when you  both got in into politics and actually  when you both were in law school was it  easy for you in those days was it  difficult do you think it's easier for  women nowadays trying to enter the  professional world so to speak well I  think it's it's just a new whole new  world compared to when I was struggling  to get into it it was really very  difficult I hate to say it but and I  won't mention the name of the law school  but I got into my law school on the  grounds that they considered me a  foreigner I got it as a foreign quota  someone in the law school had not read  up their own American history and hadn't  realized that Hawaii was annexed in 1898  and we were all American citizens but it  was very difficult getting into into  school and getting into the professions  I couldn't find a job and what all of my  contemporaries at home say oh my  goodness what you've done to politics at  holman you know wish we had never heard  of Patsy Mink  I'll say well it's because of all of  your attitudes that drove men to  politics if you give me a job when I  came home from Rossville I would have  been really happy this is the royalty  check each what would you have much the  same I am story I was married and my  husband was in the law school and for  some reason he rather that law school  seemed to think well you know he will  take care of her and it's all right in  fact they had the nerve to say in front  of the other women in the law school  there were very few  well mrs. Griffiths is the first woman  that's ever come here that didn't come  to find a husband and one of the  Schieffer girls and Whittier said well I  think you're right and as a matter of  fact I've looked them all over and I'm  thinking of transferring  I thought was very good but I originally  I you know  they had the attitude well mr. Griffiths  will see to it that she's alright she's  known being trouble which was nonsense  well you believe this is changing now oh  yes easier probably yes  I think the law schools in particular  very conscious of the fact that they  have excluded women capable intelligent  women who would otherwise qualify for  enrolment except for their sex and are  beginning to expand their admission  policies and more and more women are  having an opportunity for a professional  education in all fields not just law  what do you see as the impact not just  on women but also on men and children of  the women's movement well I think in a  way that the women's movement has been  or has been a result of the impact of  changing times that is more education  for women but more divorce in this  country women have to look at the fact  that they're going to have to be  responsible for themselves the old myths  just don't work so that I in place of it  being the women creating an impact they  are reacting to an impact I think in it  I think this is done a good deal for men  and in the country right it's given them  a greater sense of freedom and  independence well before they had this  notion that in order to be quote a  gentleman in our society they had to  have quote a dependent wife you know  that they would display at cocktail  parties and and nothing beyond that I  think now they're sensing their  responsibility and entirely different to  context and both partners in a marriage  I think are looking upon themselves as  people and trying to do the best they  can with their abilities of talents one  last question and we're running short on  time so it's a very broad question maybe  a little unfair to be asking it but how  do you feel that women in this country  compared with women abroad in terms of  rights and responsibilities  and of course I realized that we depend  on which area of the world we're talking  about but could you generalize well I  have had members of the parliamentary  body of developing nations in Africa  come in to me and say oh I can't believe  it we have more rights than the women of  America have and we were told that the  women in America had everything and of  course we have nothing like the rights  of European women I think that's  absolutely true even with the Asian and  continent so many people say to me well  with your Asian background and all the  Asian traditions how could you possibly  broken the barrier you know and I look  at them and I say really they're much  farther ahead in Asia they have some  customs that maybe you don't understand  but when you move those customs aside  but women are very dominant in Asian  cultures and lawyers and have a very  good mastery of the situation whether  they are out in the front may may be a  question or two but I think that they're  very important figures who make a  significant contribution in their  societies well thank you both so much  we've been speaking with two  distinguished United States Congress  women Patsy Mink of Hawaii and Martha  Griffiths of Michigan on the subject of  women's 